<Base title="Getting started: step by step" description="The nine steps from your first call to move-in: financing, site feasibility, design, permitting, construction, installation.">
  <div class="wrap page-title"><h1>Step-by-step</h1></div>
  <div class="wrap section" style="padding-top:8px">
    <ol class="steps">
      <li><div><h3>Financing</h3>
        <p>We refer clients to a lender. For ADUs, second-lien construction loans are available, underwritten with the rent the new unit is projected to earn. Homes built to modular code can also be financed by most banks that handle conventional construction loans.</p>
        <p>Whoever your lender is, it is important that they understand that modular draw schedules differ from site-built ones. Manufacturers usually take a draw to begin work in the factory, one or several more during construction, and a final draw before the home leaves the factory. There are also draws to the general contractor for on-site services, and the schedule needs to be coordinated with the bank.</p></div></li>
      <li><div><h3>Site feasibility</h3>
        <p>To get started, we gather information about your land to determine whether it is suitable for a modular home, and to help us in the design process. We review what you send and give you feedback that helps you decide whether to buy the land, if you don&rsquo;t own it already, and whether to move to Schematic Design.</p>
        <p><a href="/contact/">Start with your lot.</a></p></div></li>
      <li><div><h3>Schematic design</h3>
        <p>We provide a contract for Schematic Design services and collect a non-refundable deposit. The deliverables are a 2D floor plan positioned on your site and a full-color presentation of the elevation (exterior) of the home. You then sign off on Schematic Design and pay a non-refundable deposit to proceed to Detailed Design.</p></div></li>
      <li><div><h3>Detailed design</h3>
        <p>Detailed Design is where we review your scope of work, budget and schedule and reach an understanding of the project requirements. Based on the approved requirements, ma modular develops the Detailed Design. Once you approve it, we prepare Construction Documents describing what is required to build the project, and coordinate with any consultants you provide. We assist you in filing the documents required for governmental approval and in obtaining proposals and awarding contracts for construction.</p></div></li>
      <li><div><h3>Financial approval</h3>
        <p>Once the Detailed Design is complete, we submit the drawings and final cost estimate to your bank or lending institution for loan approval.</p></div></li>
      <li><div><h3>Permitting</h3>
        <p>Building permit timing varies significantly by jurisdiction. At this point your local general contractor provides a final cost estimate that includes:</p>
        <ul><li>Coordination and installation of utilities</li><li>Permit costs</li><li>Any site improvement estimates</li><li>Foundation estimate</li><li>Crane costs</li><li>&ldquo;Button-up&rdquo; cost</li></ul></div></li>
      <li><div><h3>Construction</h3>
        <p>Once the building permit and financing are in place, we can order your home and get construction under way at the manufacturer. There are two parts to the construction of your modular home: on-site work and factory work.</p>
        <p><strong>On-site work</strong> is all the preparation needed at your site to receive the modules: clearing of vegetation, grading, driveway and access preparation, construction of the foundation, and preparing the utilities to tie into the modular. Inspections by the local authority take place for this portion of the work. Time to complete site work varies with the location and complexity of your site.</p>
        <p><strong>Factory work</strong> is the construction of the modules in the manufacturing plant: all roof, floor and wall framing, windows, siding, wiring, plumbing, electrical work, HVAC, sheetrock, cabinets, tile, flooring, fixtures and paint. The modules typically arrive about 90 percent complete.</p></div></li>
      <li><div><h3>Installation</h3>
        <p>Your ma home is delivered from the manufacturer on flatbed trucks and finished on site by the local general contractor. Cranes lift the modules from the trucks, set and level them, and secure them to the foundation. &ldquo;Button-up&rdquo; crews patch the joints (sheetrock, flooring, etc.). If roofing was left off, it is completed as well. Exterior work such as decks, stairs, walkways and underpinning (if necessary) is installed.</p></div></li>
      <li><div><h3>Move-in</h3>
        <p>You perform a walk-through of the home, where we look in detail at all completed aspects of the house and create a punch list of work to be done. When the punch list is completed to your satisfaction and approved, your bank will want to do a final inspection to ensure completion. We also provide a homeowner&rsquo;s manual that identifies and explains where your breaker panel is, how to adjust your water heater and thermostat, and so on, with a list of names and contact numbers for future reference.</p></div></li>
    </ol>
  </div>
  <section class="band"><div class="wrap split"><h2>Ready to start?</h2><div><p class="lede">Send us your address and a few photos.</p><p><a class="btn" href="/contact/">Contact us</a> <a class="btn btn-quiet" href="/downloads/ma-modular-plans-and-process.pdf" download>Download the brochure (PDF)</a></p></div></div></section>
</Base>
